<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/86-bce">86 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristion was an Athenian philosopher who seized power as tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and brought the city to the side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mithridatic-war">First Mithridatic War</a>. The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> besieged Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, stormed the city on 1 March 86 BCE, and had Aristion put to death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 88 BCE, under the leadership of Athenion and then <b>Aristion</b>, Athens sided with Mithridates VI of Pontus against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
